
Explanation of Movie Recommendation Analysis Code
1. Import Dataset
In this step, the dataset is uploaded from the local system to Google Colab using the files.upload() function. The uploaded CSV file is then read using the Pandas library. The parameter on_bad_lines='skip' ensures that corrupted or improperly formatted rows are ignored.
The df.head() function displays the first five rows of the dataset, helping to verify that the data has been loaded correctly.
Purpose:
Load dataset into the environment
Understand the basic structure of the data
2. Dataset Overview
This section provides an overall understanding of the dataset.
df.shape shows the number of rows and columns.
df.columns lists all column names.
df.info() displays data types, non-null counts, and memory usage.
df.describe() gives statistical summaries such as mean, median, minimum, and maximum for numerical columns.
Purpose:
Understand dataset size and structure
Identify numerical and categorical features
3. Missing Values & Duplicate Handling
Here, missing values and duplicate records are identified.
df.isnull().sum() counts missing values in each column.
df.duplicated().sum() identifies duplicate rows.
Purpose:
Detect data quality issues
Prepare for data cleaning
4. Data Cleaning & Preprocessing
This step ensures the dataset is clean and usable.
Text Data Handling
Missing values in textual columns such as genre, keywords, overview, cast, and director are filled with empty strings. This prevents errors during text processing and NLP operations.
Numerical Data Handling
Missing values in budget, revenue, and runtime are filled using the median value to reduce the impact of outliers.
Duplicate Removal
Duplicate rows are removed using drop_duplicates().
After cleaning, missing values are rechecked to confirm successful preprocessing.
Purpose:
Improve data quality
Ensure consistency for analysis and modeling
5. Exploratory Data Analysis (EDA)
EDA is performed to understand patterns and relationships in the data.
Key Visualizations:
Histogram: Distribution of movie popularity
Scatter Plot: Budget vs Revenue and Budget vs Popularity
Heatmap: Correlation among numerical features
Line Plot: Number of movies released per year
Box Plot: Runtime distribution
Bar Charts: Top directors and genres
Pie Chart: Top movie languages
Pair Plot: Relationship between key numerical variables
Insights:
Movies with higher budgets often generate higher revenue.
Popularity is influenced by vote count and ratings.
Certain genres and directors dominate movie production.
Presence of outliers in budget and popularity.
Purpose:
Identify trends, correlations, and anomalies
Support feature selection and modeling decisions
6. Feature Engineering
Feature engineering improves model performance.
Combined Text Features
Multiple textual attributes (genre, keywords, overview, cast, director) are merged into a single feature called combined_features.
TF-IDF Vectorization
TF-IDF converts text into numerical vectors by assigning importance to words based on frequency.
Purpose:
Convert unstructured text into machine-readable form
Improve recommendation accuracy
7. Content-Based Recommendation System
A content-based recommendation system is built using:
Cosine Similarity: Measures similarity between movies based on TF-IDF vectors.
Recommendation Function: Returns the top N movies similar to a given movie title.
If the movie title is not found, an appropriate message is returned.
Purpose:
Recommend movies based on content similarity
Personalize user experience
8. Machine Learning: Popularity Prediction
This section predicts movie popularity.
Outlier Removal
Outliers in popularity are removed using the IQR method.
Feature Transformation
Log transformation is applied to budget and revenue.
A weighted vote score is calculated.
Target Variable
Movie popularity is transformed using logarithmic scaling to reduce skewness.
Purpose:
Prepare clean and normalized data for regression modeling
9. Model Training & Evaluation
Model Used
Random Forest Regressor is chosen due to:
Ability to handle non-linearity
Robustness to outliers
High predictive accuracy
Evaluation Metrics
Mean Squared Error (MSE)
R² Score
Feature importance is calculated to identify the most influential predictors.
Purpose:
Train a reliable regression model
Measure model performance
10. Cross-Validation
5-fold cross-validation is applied using negative mean squared error as the scoring metric. This ensures the model’s performance is stable and not dependent on a single train-test split.
Purpose:
Improve model reliability
Prevent overfitting
11. Feature Importance
The importance of each feature used in the Random Forest model is displayed. This helps in understanding which variables contribute most to popularity prediction.
Purpose:
Model interpretability
Feature selection insights
12. Deployment – Gradio Web Application
A Gradio-based web interface is created to deploy the system.
Features:
Movie selection dropdown
Adjustable number of recommendations
Movie overview display
Recommended movies with similarity scores
Predicted popularity output
The application provides an interactive and user-friendly interface.
Purpose:
Deploy the model for real-world usage
Enable non-technical users to interact with the system
Conclusion
This project successfully integrates EDA, NLP-based recommendation, machine learning regression, and deployment into a single end-to-end system. The model demonstrates strong performance and practical relevance for movie recommendation platforms.
